\title{Calibration PipeOp}
\description{
Calibrates a probabilistic \code{\link[mlr3:Learner]{Learner}},
\code{\link[mlr3pipelines:mlr_learners_graph]{GraphLearner}}, or bare
\code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}} (converted with \code{\link[mlr3:as_learner]{mlr3::as_learner()}}).

Both strategies fit the calibrator on predictions held out from model training
(Niculescu-Mizil A, Caruana R (2005).
\dQuote{Predicting Good Probabilities with Supervised Learning.}
In \emph{Proceedings of the 22nd International Conference on Machine Learning}, 625-632.
\doi{10.1145/1102351.1102430}.):
\itemize{
\item \code{strategy = "oof"}: one calibrator on pooled out-of-fold predictions; one uncalibrated
model refitted on the full task and calibrated at prediction time
(scikit-learn \code{CalibratedClassifierCV} with \code{ensemble = FALSE}; Pedregosa F, Varoquaux G, Gramfort A, Michel V, Thirion B, Grisel O, Blondel M, Prettenhofer P, Weiss R, Dubourg V, Vanderplas J, Passos A, Cournapeau D, Brucher M, Perrot M, Duchesnay É (2011).
\dQuote{Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python.}
\emph{Journal of Machine Learning Research}, \bold{12}(85), 2825-2830.
See also the probability calibration user guide: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html, \url{https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/pedregosa11a.html}.).
\item \code{strategy = "per_fold"}: one calibrator per fold; fold-wise calibrated probabilities are averaged
(\code{ensemble = TRUE}).
}

When the PipeOp runs an uninstantiated resampling itself (no precomputed \code{rr}), it clones the task and sets the
target \code{"stratum"} role before \code{resample()}, which helps avoid missing-class folds while keeping
the requested scheme and fold count.
A pre-instantiated resampling is cloned and its stored train/test splits are reused unchanged.
Infeasible class counts still fail with a diagnostic.
A supplied \code{\link[mlr3:ResampleResult]{ResampleResult}} is validated and reused as-is
(never rerun, re-stratified, or overwritten).
Repeated or overlapping test predictions, as produced by repeated cross-validation or bootstrap,
are averaged by task row before fitting the pooled \code{"oof"} calibrator.
Rows without any test prediction are omitted from that fit.
\code{predict_type} is fixed to \code{"prob"}.

Calibrators are \link{Calibrator} prototypes.
Wrapper-style parameter ids expose both the learner and calibrator for outer \code{AutoTuner} use.
Multiclass calibration requires a calibrator with the \code{"multiclass"} property.
\code{AutoTuner} is not accepted as \code{learner}; wrap the calibrated object in an outer \code{AutoTuner},
or use \code{\link[=tune_then_calibrate]{tune_then_calibrate()}} for sequential tuning then calibration.
Use \code{clb("selector")} to tune over several calibrators.
Put preprocessing that should enter the calibration resampling inside the wrapped \code{learner},
not upstream of \code{po("calibrate")}.
}
\examples{
if (mlr3misc::require_namespaces("mlbench", quietly = TRUE) &&
  mlr3misc::require_namespaces("mlr3learners", quietly = TRUE) &&
  mlr3misc::require_namespaces("rpart", quietly = TRUE)) {
  set.seed(1)

  data("Sonar", package = "mlbench")
  task = mlr3::as_task_classif(Sonar, target = "Class", positive = "M")
  learner_cal = mlr3::as_learner(mlr3pipelines::po(
    "calibrate",
    learner = mlr3::lrn("classif.rpart", predict_type = "prob"),
    calibrator = clb("platt"),
    strategy = "oof",
    resampling = mlr3::rsmp("cv", folds = 5)
  ))

  learner_cal$train(task)
  learner_cal$predict(task)$score(mlr3::msr("classif.bbrier"))
}

